# cspell:words selfhost precompiler obfuscat Obfuscator lldb bijective dartbug
#
# gen_snapshot learns --load-obfuscation-map, so an obfuscated release is
# patchable at all.
#
# WHY. The CLI already passes the release's own map to the patch build
# (`--obfuscate --load-obfuscation-map=<map> --strip`) and its tests already
# assert it (ios_patcher_test.dart:924, :950). Our gen_snapshot advertised
# --save-obfuscation-map ONLY, so the AOT step exited 255 long before Route B
# was reached. The bug was entirely below the CLI.
#
# THE CONTRACT WAS RECOVERED, NOT INVENTED. Every user-visible string below is
# reproduced verbatim from the previous pin's Shorebird fork binary, which does
# carry the flag:
#   ~/.shorebird/bin/cache/flutter/309dd6573a9fe716410489284cd325a34b950375/
#     bin/cache/artifacts/engine/ios-release/gen_snapshot_arm64
#   $ strings -a <that> | grep -i obfuscat | sort -u
# yielding, among others:
#   --load_obfuscation_map=
#   --load_obfuscation_map=%s
#   Empty value for option load_obfuscation_map
#   --load-obfuscation-map=<...> should only be specified when obfuscation is
#       enabled by the --obfuscate flag.
#   Obfuscation map is already initialized.
#   Could not load obfuscation map: file callbacks not set
#   Could not open obfuscation map file: %s
#   Invalid obfuscation map: expected '['
#   Invalid obfuscation map: expected '"'
#   Invalid obfuscation map: unterminated string
#   Invalid obfuscation map: odd number of entries (expected pairs)
#   Path to a JSON obfuscation map file to load before kernel translation.
#       Used for consistent obfuscation across patch builds.
#   [--load-obfuscation-map=<map-filename>]
#   ... plus the replacement help paragraph, reproduced here word for word.
#
# THE REFERENCE'S ARCHITECTURE, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Two controls
# against our own binary settle how that binary was built:
#   * `--load_obfuscation_map=%s` appears in the reference and NO `--<flag>=%s`
#     string appears in ours. That is a format used to forward the option into
#     vm_options, i.e. the reference carried the option as a VM FLAG, and
#     "Path to a JSON obfuscation map file..." is that flag's DEFINE_FLAG
#     description.
#   * `Dart_GetObfuscationMap` appears as a string in BOTH binaries (so it comes
#     from a macro, not from a new API), and no Dart_*Obfuscation* Load/Set
#     symbol appears in either. The reference added NO new embedder API.
# This patch therefore CONVERGES with the reference: VM flag, file read through
# the embedder file callbacks, hand-written parser in the VM. The H4 plan
# predicted a divergence here (it proposed parsing in bin/ and adding
# Dart_LoadObfuscationMap); that shape is refuted below.
#
# WHY NOT A Dart_* API CALLED FROM bin/ -- MEASURED, NOT REASONED. The
# obfuscation state is seeded during isolate bootstrap. Under lldb, on the host
# gen_snapshot, breaking on Obfuscator::InitializeRenamingMap:
#   frame #0  dart::Obfuscator::InitializeRenamingMap()
#   frame #1  dart::Obfuscator::Obfuscator(dart::Thread*, dart::String const&)
#   frame #2  dart::BootstrapFromKernelSingleProgram(...)
#   frame #3  dart::Object::Init(dart::IsolateGroup*, ...)
#   frame #4  dart::CreateIsolate(...)
#   frame #5  Dart_CreateIsolateGroupFromKernel
#   frame #6  dart::bin::main(int, char**)
# and Dart_Precompile had not yet been reached. By the time bin/ regains
# control the map exists and kernel translation has begun issuing renames
# (kernel_translation_helper.cc builds an Obfuscator in seven places). An
# embedder-side load could therefore only MERGE into a live map -- it would
# accept the flag and then rename INCONSISTENTLY, which is worse than refusing
# it, because the patch would build, ship, and bind to names the release does
# not have. The only correct seam is the fresh-start branch of the Obfuscator
# constructor, which is where this patch loads.
#
# THE CURSOR. The JSON map carries PAIRS ONLY; the saved state also carries the
# rename cursor name_ (char name_[100], precompiler.h). NewAtomicRename's
# do/while rejects only IDENTITY renames -- it does not reject a name already in
# use as a VALUE -- so a load that leaves the cursor at zero restarts at 'a' and
# hands a second identifier a name the release already spent.
#
# The cursor is reconstructed as the greatest generated value in the loaded map.
# Read off NextName(): inc(a)=b .. inc(z)=A .. inc(Z)=a & carry, a fresh
# position starts at 'a', and name_[0] is the LEAST significant digit. The
# renames are therefore a bijective base-52 numeral written little-endian, and
# the H4 plan's proposed rule ("greatest value that is entirely [a-z], by length
# then lexicographic") is wrong three times over:
#   * the alphabet is [a-zA-Z]; restricting to lowercase discards every value
#     carrying an uppercase digit;
#   * the most significant digit is the LAST character, so plain lexicographic
#     comparison reads the numeral backwards;
#   * identity renames (key == value, e.g. "dynamic") are not generated names
#     and must be excluded, or the cursor is pushed arbitrarily far.
# Simulated against the generator, the plan's rule lands 6 names early at 500
# generated names, 10 early at 3,000, and 2,398 early at 20,000 -- and release
# 35's real map has 19,830 pairs. Each early name is already bound to a
# different identifier.
#
# CANONICALIZATION IS LOAD-BEARING AND SILENT. ObfuscationMapTraits hashes on
# String CONTENT but matches on POINTER IDENTITY (IsMatch is `a.ptr() ==
# b.ptr()`). A map loaded as plain Strings would hash into the correct buckets
# and never match, producing a table that looks populated and renames as if it
# were empty. Every loaded key and value is interned with Symbols::New.
#
# --obfuscation_cursor_mode IS AN INSTRUMENT, NOT A KNOB. Mode 1 (correct) is
# the default and the only setting that ships behaviour. Modes 0 (no restore)
# and 2 (the refuted lowercase rule) exist so that
# probes/g43_obfuscation_map_load.sh can demonstrate it can SEE the failure it
# claims to test -- and, specifically, that it distinguishes "correct" from
# "almost correct", not merely "present" from "absent".
#
# THE FOUR SEAMS, as measured in our tree (the plan's first-draft anchors are
# corrected here):
#   runtime/bin/gen_snapshot.cc:117      STRING_OPTIONS_LIST entry
